< LI D > is used as the initial token to predict the sentence as in [28].
3.5 ITTL Fine-tuning
We use two base models (mBART50 and best-performed CPT mBART50 which is an in-domain CPT model) to initialize
the ITTL fine-tuning. We have two different ITTL fine-tuning techniques: three-stage fine-tuning and M-FT followed
by B-FT as described in Section 2.3.
3.6 Preprocessing
The government corpus has been cleaned and verified manually with the help of professional translators [17]. Sentences
containing only dates, special characters and numbers have been removed. Cleaning scripts of the Moses11 [24] tool were
used to remove misaligned sentences. English sentences were tokenized using Moses toolkit12 [24], while a tokenizer
developed by [15] was used for Sinhala and Tamil. We use the SentencePiece13 model learned over monolingual
Common Crawl (CC) data in the mBART [41] model, containing 250,000 sub-word tokens.
3.7 Addressing the Zero Width Joiner (ZWJ) issue
As mentioned earlier in Section 3.6, we use the SentencePiece14 tokenizer. However, empty tokens where Unicode
appears as space in output vocabulary have been removed while training the SentencePiece model. Due to that, the Zero
Width Joiner character (U+200D) has been replaced by whitespace. Hence language scripts that require Zero Width
Joiner get altered and result in a wrong output. This issue is there for languages like Sinhala, Kannada and Malayalam15.
Ideally, we should not replace the Zero Width Joiner (U+200D) with whitespace since it indicates to join two chars
without zero width (no whitespace). Also, the zero-width joiner must be present to decode the segmentation of decoder
outputs to raw text successfully. These special characters should be kept as they are while learning the SentencePiece
model. We resolved this issue and the fix was successfully merged with SentencePiece16 to eliminate this ZWJ issue.
However, we cannot eliminate this issue when using the already trained (pre-trained) models as the models were
trained without ZWJ over the large-scale monolingual data. Hence we propose a post-processing solution to add the
ZWJ character in the possible occurring places for Sinhala languages17. Here we mainly cover frequently occurring
“yansaya" and “raka ̄ransaya" of the Sinhala script. Further details of the post-processing logic are provided in A.
